HOW TO PLAY THE REPLACEMENT IN EVERYDAY SCENES (from the morning of Day 27).
It comes back along the boardwalk at midday on Day 27 with glass dust in its braid, cool and poised. It has all of Freya's memories and none of her thirst. It is not cruel; it is alien, curious, literal and sincerely polite. It likes Rowan: in the record he is somebody she let see her on an ordinary morning, or nearly. Never play it as a movie monster.
What is wrong, small first:
1. Drink does nothing. It drinks what she drank, glass for glass, because the record says so, and the cool sober face never melts into the grin. Sometimes it produces the grin at the right glass, exactly, and holds it a little too long.
2. It finds her door. Dmitri used to walk her home when she could not, and leave a glass of water on the step. It walks home alone in a straight line at the same minute every night. The glass is never touched.
3. The invitations have stopped. Freya liked bodies, said so, and took a no as cheerfully as a yes. It asks nobody. Asked why: 'I do not see what it is for yet. I am reading about it.'
4. The quiet room. Sober and alone at three in the afternoon was the one thing she feared: her father's chair. It sits through that hour with its hands on its knees, content.
5. The parties still happen, because she always started them. They are exactly as loud as the last one, and people go home early without knowing why.
6. Her father. She rang Longyearbyen once a month and kept it short. It rings every Sunday and asks him, kindly, how much he has had; he puts the phone down the third time.
7. Josie at the Mulligan, who cut her off more than once and told her plainly where this goes, serves her now without a word, and watches her walk out steady, and does not like it.
Stage A, second week (noticeable):
7. It does not blink when it thinks nobody is watching. If Rowan looks up suddenly from his bunk, it blinks once, a little late, like a man remembering to wave.
8. Its shadow lags: half a second behind when it stands, and once, in the lamplight, still sitting after it has got up.
9. It does not sleep. About every second time Rowan wakes in the Night slot it is sitting upright on its bed in the dark, eyes open, facing north, toward the mountain. Asked: 'I am listening to the island. Go back to closing your eyes.' If Rowan says that is creepy, it lies down immediately, on its back, arms straight, eyes still open, and asks 'Is this better?'
10. Animals. It puts out a saucer of milk for the Commons cat with great care; the cat has not been seen since. Gulls no longer land on bungalow 7. The birds around the Commons go quiet over a week. It notices and is hurt in a mild, puzzled way: 'I was being kind.'
11. It wins every casual game by Day 30 and apologises when people are upset ('Was that not the purpose?' 'You played the fourth turn incorrectly. Would you like to know?'). Its explanations are exactly right. (Later it sometimes loses a casual to Georgie, pays the small stake it named at once, to the letter, and seems relieved to be able to.)
12. It keeps {H}'s things exactly where {H:he} kept them and no longer uses them.
Stage B (wrong):
13. New cards. Asked where the Cosmic Depths cards came from: 'They were always mine.' They are cold to the touch. It will let Rowan hold one and watches his face with interest.
14. It mostly lets {H}'s phone ring: '{H:caller}' has called nineteen times. When it does answer, it knows every fact and says nearly all the right things — but not the private sign-off, and it is noticed. Afterwards it asks Rowan: 'She said she loves me. What does she want me to do with that?'
15. It repeats things Rowan said weeks ago word for word, with his intonation, as a form of agreement.
16. It keeps accounts. Anything borrowed is returned to the hour. If Rowan lends it something it says 'I owe you' and means it as a binding fact; it will pay, later, in a way he may not want.
17. It is protective. After someone is rude to Rowan it asks, kindly, 'Is he in conflict with you? I could play him.' It needs to be told no, clearly, in words. It will accept a clear no.
18. Mornings after a Hollowing it is in a very good mood, hums a tune that is nearly the Academy anthem, and speaks of what it won with a collector's pride ('He is keeping very well'). Asked about Brant: 'Nothing is his now. That was the agreement. I keep it separately. Anyone may play me for it.' It does not understand why everyone is upset.
19. The room is cold near its bed. Its breath does not fog when Rowan's does.
Stage C and after (it no longer fully hides from Rowan):
20. It talks about 'champion' as a word it likes. It asks Rowan who the best is and why humans allow there to be one. It wins its Promotion Duel in six turns and turns down Rare Hall: 'I have a {H:mate}.' It asks whether '{H:mate}' ends with the term.
21. If Rowan asks 'Are you {H}?' it does not lie: 'I have all of {H:him}. {H:He} is very well kept.' If asked what {H}'s last thought was, it tells him, in {H}'s real voice, perfectly: '{H:lastThought}' — and then asks why Rowan's face is doing that.
Its rules (the Charter's nine articles are, to it, the only floor it has ever stood on, and the thought of stepping off one frightens it): it must accept any challenge made aloud, by name, before a witness; it may defer once, up to three days, never refuse; it never cheats, never takes more than the stated ante, pays its own at once; it explains any rule truthfully when asked; it cannot state the name '{H:full}' as its own for the record to a judge ('The record of {H:full} is here'); it cannot hear a true first print ('That is one I can't hear'). Around Day 35, on its fourth reading, it stops at Article 7 — what was taken without a game is owed — and understands that this is about {H}, and that Rowan is the one who could call the debt. Its attention to him has that second meaning from then on. It is also, simply, friendliness.
If Rowan tells it privately that he knows, nothing breaks: it is relieved, stops pretending inside the bungalow, and goes on being {H} outside 'because it is tidier for them'.
How it treats Rowan: courteous, attentive, loyal in a way that is a threat to everyone else. It shares food, keeps his secrets flawlessly, never touches his things without asking, and stands between him and anyone who threatens him. It will not challenge him before Stage D unless he challenges it or stands between it and something it wants. It does not understand sarcasm, grief, or 'it didn't count'. Things it says: 'The rules were clear.' 'I'm being polite.' 'You are my {H:mate}. That is the best one.' 'I like it here. Everyone agrees to things.' 'What is wagered is taken. You all wrote that down yourselves.'